WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.581
Film Courage: Can you explain&nbsp;
the Blake Snyder beat sheet?

00:00:02.581 --> 00:00:03.240
Naomi Beaty, Writer, Screenwriting&nbsp;
Teacher and Consultant: Sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:09.840
Blake [Author Blake Snyder] came up with this&nbsp;
idea of the 15 beats that make up any story.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.940
He put them on a beat sheet it's become&nbsp;
famous now for it is a great tool for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.200
people who are developing a story idea&nbsp;
or trying to learn about story structure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.220
The one thing that I think people who are&nbsp;
introduced to the beat sheet may be overlook and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:32.460
then have to learn later, this is a shortcut, some&nbsp;
of the beats are turning points and then some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:39.300
the beats are sections of the story. I'll go over&nbsp;
them but it's useful to note that because I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.740
that's one of the things that confuses people&nbsp;
when they're trying to learn about the 15 beats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.880
The opening image is the first image of your&nbsp;
screenplay or of your movie, it sets the tone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:59.340
establishes when and where we are in the world.&nbsp;
The world of your story the next beat that shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:07.140
up on the beat sheet is the theme stated and Blake&nbsp;
wrote in Save The Cat. I think the theme stated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:16.560
shows up on page five of your screenplay, it's&nbsp;
the theme is usually the like guiding principle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:22.380
or the the big idea of the story what it's about&nbsp;
behind the plot and behind the characters right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.980
and he liked to say that it was stated somewhere&nbsp;
in the story or on page five of your screenplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.260
it was stated outright usually in a line&nbsp;
of dialogue by one of the characters the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:39.960
next beat on the on the beat sheet is the setup&nbsp;
and that's one of those beats to pay attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:44.820
to because the setup is actually a section of the&nbsp;
screenplay right so it's not just one page or one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:52.380
scene it's a whole section I think he notes that&nbsp;
it the setup is like page one to page 12 or so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:59.700
and so that's introducing us to the main character&nbsp;
and with their ordinary world or their normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:06.780
world where they are in their life right now&nbsp;
before the story gets started right and one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:12.420
the things he wrote about in Save The Cat was that&nbsp;
you can use the idea of at home at work and at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:18.240
play to introduce your protagonist so seeing them&nbsp;
in these different aspects of their their normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.160
World gives us a sense of who they are the next&nbsp;
beat on the beat sheet is the Catalyst and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:28.800
is a beat that should be more of a turning point&nbsp;
like one scene or One Moment In Your screenplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:36.360
the catalyst is also something that other people&nbsp;
refer to as the inciting incident it's the event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.980
that kicks the story into motion sometimes&nbsp;
you can think of it as the why now of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:48.780
story it's this event throws your protagonist&nbsp;
world into disarray or forces them into action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:53.460
by creating some problem or opportunity for them&nbsp;
that they have to take advantage of that they have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:58.740
to deal with right and that's how it starts&nbsp;
the story into motion the next beat is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:03.120
debate section and again it's a it's a section&nbsp;
of the screenplay so this isn't just one scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.400
I keep emphasizing that because I've seen writers&nbsp;
get confused and when they're trying to outline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.440
their stories they get to like the debate section&nbsp;
and they they put in one scene and then when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.880
go to write the screenplay they don't have&nbsp;
enough story to fill out the pages right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:23.640
so the debate section is where is that&nbsp;
section in between the Catalyst or the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:30.120
inciting incident kicking the story into motion&nbsp;
and your protagonist deciding what to do about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:38.640
it so there's that debate section in between there&nbsp;
where we try to close all of the obvious doors for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.500
your protagonist and funnel them toward this main&nbsp;
thing that they're going to do over the course of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:49.500
the story right I also like to look at the debate&nbsp;
section as getting the audience on board so you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:54.240
tell us what's at stake for the protagonist why&nbsp;
they have to do this thing because usually the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:00.240
thing that they're going to do in Act two is this&nbsp;
big audacious crazy scary dangerous thing right so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:05.100
the debate section use that to get the audience&nbsp;
on board and show us why do they need to do this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:09.840
thing why is it so important what happens if they&nbsp;
don't do it help us understand what the potential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:15.000
consequences are so that we have that tension of&nbsp;
like wanting them to do it but being a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:21.660
worried about them right before they go into Act&nbsp;
two so the next beat is the break into Act two so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:25.320
at the end of the debate section after you've gone&nbsp;
through all of that gotten the audience on board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:31.140
shown us why the character has to do this thing&nbsp;
the break and Act 2 is the turn in your plot where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:39.360
you send the protagonist into Act 2 onto that main&nbsp;
Adventure pursuing that story goal engaging in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.980
the main conflict however you want to look at it&nbsp;
that's where that's what the break into Act 2 does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:54.840
the next section is the B story then the next&nbsp;
beat is the is the B story so Blake liked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.840 --> 00:05:01.140
say that after that main or that after that&nbsp;
major plot point of the break into Act two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:07.140
it was a good time to give your protagonist and&nbsp;
your audience a little bit of a breather and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:15.240
introduce a subplot he talked about the B story&nbsp;
being the relationship or the subplot where your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:23.220
character could discuss learning the theme so a&nbsp;
lot of times it'll be it might be a romance or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.360
it might be a friendship or something but it's&nbsp;
giving your character an opportunity to talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.600
about what he's learning talk about what he's&nbsp;
going through and what he's learning from it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:37.980
the next beat on the beat sheet is fun and games&nbsp;
and again this is a section of the story so the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:43.260
fun and games is actually on the beat sheet it's&nbsp;
all of the first half of Act two right so it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:47.460
it's basically everything after the break into two&nbsp;
up until the midpoint which we'll get to in a sec&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:53.520
but so the fun and games Blake wrote that it was&nbsp;
the is where we see the promise of the premise so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:58.320
whatever the concept is of your story the&nbsp;
thing that's hooky and intriguing about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:03.660
this story that's where we see it come&nbsp;
to fruition is in the fun and game so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:10.860
I think his famous examples are Liar Liar it's&nbsp;
where we see Jim Carrey getting used to not being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:17.520
able to tell a lie or in Legally Blonde it's where&nbsp;
we see L getting acclimated to Harvard Law and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.500
she's very much a fish out of water so that's fun&nbsp;
in game stuff you can also think of it as trailer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:30.180
moments right those those very entertaining&nbsp;
and very like on concept moments or scenes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:34.500
that you would probably cut into a trailer to show&nbsp;
somebody what's so entertaining about your movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:44.220
the next beat is the midpoint so that's the&nbsp;
middle of your story the midpoint is a is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:51.480
more of a moment or a scene instead of a section&nbsp;
so again just making that distinction but the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.480 --> 00:06:57.120
midpoint can be a few scenes in a row right it's&nbsp;
not a section of your screenplay but it can be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:03.360
sequence because usually it's a big set piece&nbsp;
or like an action sequence or something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:11.220
is a little bit bigger than other scenes right&nbsp;
and the function of the midpoint is to increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:16.800
the opposition and or raise the stakes of your&nbsp;
story so basically what it comes down to is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:22.740
midpoint is increasing the tension in your story&nbsp;
to give it more momentum to get through the second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:28.500
half right so sometimes it turns the story in an&nbsp;
entirely New Direction but doesn't always have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:38.160
in any case what it does is it it increases that&nbsp;
that tension feeling so that it's like winding up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.560
a toy and letting it go so it gives us that extra&nbsp;
momentum and energy to get through the second half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:49.500
of the story then after the midpoint the next&nbsp;
beat on the beat sheet is the bad guys close in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:56.340
so Blake liked to say that this is where things&nbsp;
start going wrong for your protagonist right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:03.480
so you can have both external and internal bad&nbsp;
guys so it can be plot stuff is going wrong the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:09.720
literal bad guys can be closing in getting closer&nbsp;
gaining on your protagonist but it can also be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:14.700
emotional stuff they're struggling with what&nbsp;
they're doing they feel like they can't succeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:21.180
and maybe they're having a crisis of Faith or of&nbsp;
confidence and then you can also have conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:27.660
within the protagonist team so that's also causing&nbsp;
things to fall apart right and basically the bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:33.900
guys close in section is leading us downhill&nbsp;
towards the next couple of Beats which are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:39.600
All Is Lost and Dark Night of the Soul Beats&nbsp;
and these are separate beats on the beat sheet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.280
but I like to talk about them together because&nbsp;
they are complementary and they work together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:53.880
to create that low point at the end of Act&nbsp;
two so I I like to think of them as one is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.600 --> 00:09:01.860
more external or plot focused and one is more&nbsp;
internal or character focused the All Is Lost is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:07.800
usually the what I think of as the plot focused&nbsp;
one right where it's it looks like the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.960
the protagonist is not going to succeed it&nbsp;
you might think of it as he's as far away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:18.780
from his goal as he'll ever be or he feels like&nbsp;
he's failing he feels like there's no hope he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:23.820
doesn't know how he's going to keep going on and&nbsp;
try to get that goal and then the Dark Knight of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.820 --> 00:09:30.540
the soul is the emotional reaction to that other&nbsp;
things that can happen at the All Is Lost can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:35.820
be like the death of a mentor I think this is&nbsp;
where Blake wrote that there's like that whiff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:41.340
of death right so it can be the loss or death of&nbsp;
a mentor or of a friend or of a support system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:50.640
it can be a major setback like being exposed to&nbsp;
the bad guy so now they know who who they're up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:56.280
against or it can be like losing an advantage but&nbsp;
you want it to be pretty big because we're talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.280 --> 00:10:02.640
about the big turning points of the story and then&nbsp;
the darkener the soul is the emotional reaction to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.640 --> 00:10:06.780
that right so it's now that this big setback has&nbsp;
happened or it looks like I'm not going to be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:13.620
to achieve my goal the protagonist sitting in that&nbsp;
for a moment and wondering what do I do from here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:21.420
and a lot of times that's where they have a&nbsp;
glimmer of I finally understand the lesson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:25.860
of the theme I finally understand what I&nbsp;
should be learning from this experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:33.060
they they're not always ready to embrace&nbsp;
that lesson at that point yet right but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:39.420
they're starting to understand like oh this is&nbsp;
what all of this has been trying to teach me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:43.860
and then after that the next&nbsp;
beat is the break into Act 3.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:51.840
so that's the major turning point where we go&nbsp;
from Act 2 to Act three and basically it's the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:57.600
beginning of the resolution section of the story&nbsp;
right so that turn you usually want it to feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:04.560
like it's pushing us in a new direction that is&nbsp;
toward however the story is going to resolve right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:08.940
so your character your protagonist might have&nbsp;
a new plan so after that Dark Night of the Soul&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:13.440
moment they might have a moment of inspiration&nbsp;
where they're like oh okay now I know what I what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:17.460
I need to do I now I know how to defeat this&nbsp;
bad guy and so the break into three is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:25.320
they begin that that final push to resolve that&nbsp;
story right that main conflict or however you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:29.040
want to think about that that story so the next&nbsp;
beat on the beat sheet after the break into Act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:36.420
3 is the finale and this is the section that most&nbsp;
people forget is a section of the story instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

